      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Given the function \\( z = x^2 + 2y^2 + 6x - 4y + 22 \\), we need to find its minimum value. To do this, we will complete the square for both the \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) terms.\n\nFirst, consider the \\( x \\)-terms:\n\\[ x^2 + 6x. \\]\nTo complete the square, we add and subtract \\((\\frac{6}{2})^2 = 9\\):\n\\[ x^2 + 6x = (x + 3)^2 - 9. \\]\n\nNext, consider the \\( y \\)-terms:\n\\[ 2y^2 - 4y. \\]\nTo complete the square, we first factor out the 2:\n\\[ 2(y^2 - 2y). \\]\nThen we add and subtract \\((\\frac{-2}{2})^2 = 1\\):\n\\[ y^2 - 2y = (y - 1)^2 - 1, \\]\nso\n\\[ 2(y^2 - 2y) = 2((y - 1)^2 - 1) = 2(y - 1)^2 - 2. \\]\n\nNow substitute these completed squares back into the original equation:\n\\[ z = (x + 3)^2 - 9 + 2(y - 1)^2 - 2 + 22. \\]\nSimplify the expression:\n\\[ z = (x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2 + 11. \\]\n\nSince \\((x + 3)^2 \\geq 0\\) and \\(2(y - 1)^2 \\geq 0\\) for all real numbers \\(x\\) and \\(y\\), the minimum value of \\(z\\) occurs when both \\((x + 3)^2 = 0\\) and \\(2(y - 1)^2 = 0\\), i.e., when \\(x = -3\\) and \\(y = 1\\). Substituting these values into the equation for \\(z\\), we get:\n\\[ z = 0 + 0 + 11 = 11. \\]\n\nThus, the minimum value of \\(z\\) is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).",
